{% set local_problem %}
<p>Arab sits at the edge of the Appalachian Plateau, surrounded by beautiful hardwood forests mixed with pines. Those trees provide shade and scenery — but they also drop an enormous amount of debris onto your roof and into your gutters. Oak leaves pile up in fall. Pine needles accumulate year-round. Maple seeds, twigs, and pollen add to the mix spring through fall.</p>

<p>Without gutter guards, Arab homeowners face a never-ending maintenance cycle. Gutters clog, water overflows, and you're back on the ladder again — if you can safely reach them. On hillside lots, clogged gutters are especially dangerous. Overflow water doesn't just drip down your siding — it concentrates and accelerates toward your foundation at roughly 1.5x normal velocity on sloped terrain.</p>

<p>Many Arab homeowners tell us they clean gutters 3-4 times per year, or pay $150-200 each time for professional cleaning. That's $600-800 annually for a chore that never ends. Over 4 years, that's $2,400-$3,200 — more than quality guards that solve the problem permanently.</p>

<p><strong>Our strong opinion:</strong> Don't buy foam gutter inserts from the hardware store. We've pulled thousands of feet of failed foam out of Marshall County gutters. They decompose in Alabama humidity, trap debris inside instead of keeping it out, and become part of the clog within 18-24 months. Spend on micro-mesh or don't bother at all.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are protective covers fitting over or inside your gutters. They allow water to enter while blocking leaves, pine needles, twigs, and debris. The debris either blows away or sits on top where it eventually dries and falls off.</p>

<p><strong>What works in Arab:</strong> Micro-mesh guards with surgical-grade stainless steel screen. Holes around 50 microns — small enough to block even fine pine needles that slip through standard screen guards. Water sheets through; debris stays on top.</p>

<p><strong>What doesn't work here:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Foam inserts — decompose in humidity within 18-24 months, become part of the clog</li>
<li>Plastic snap-in screens — warp in summer heat, don't block fine debris like pine needles</li>
<li>Brush-style guards — pine needles embed in bristles, nearly impossible to clean out</li>
</ul>

<p>For Arab homes on steep terrain where water hits gutters with more force, we ensure guards can handle the flow rate. Some cheaper guards restrict water entry too much — fine on flat roofs, problematic on mountain pitches. We match guard selection to your specific roof angles.</p>

<p><strong>The realistic expectation:</strong> Quality guards reduce cleaning 90%+, not 100%. You might brush surface debris off once a year. That's dramatically better than scooping decomposed sludge four times yearly, but it's not "never touch them again."</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed guards on many <a href="/recent-projects/">Arab homes</a> and understand the specific debris challenges of the mountain area — pine needles, heavy oak leaves, and steep roof pitches that complicate guard performance. We'll recommend the right solution for your specific situation, not push whatever has the best margin.</p>

<p>Based just 30 minutes away in Huntsville, we stand behind our work. If something isn't performing, we're close enough to address it.</p>

<p><strong>Warning:</strong> Be skeptical of gutter guard companies offering "lifetime" cleaning guarantees. Read the fine print. Most exclude "maintenance debris" — defined broadly enough to cover almost everything. We're upfront: guards reduce cleaning 90%+, not 100%. Occasional surface maintenance happens. That's the honest reality.</p>

